{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_1681810333#3_3172669123","relevance":2,"reason":"The review is about fatal anaphylaxis including venom/insect sting triggers and says quality-of-life impairment should be considered in patients at risk for anaphylaxis, but it is not dedicated specifically to bee stings or bee-sting allergy quality of life.","learned":"For patients at risk of venom/insect-sting anaphylaxis, quality-of-life impairment is relevant to treatment decisions."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1077","reason":"The broad venom allergy quality-of-life query may still yield additional dedicated bee/Hymenoptera sting allergy sources after the already skipped known results."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1077"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_32_853332099#11_1643570592","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1077","rank":12,"snippet":"dust mites. Skin testing may help diagnose food allergies as well. Allergy skin tests are widely used to help diagnose allergic conditions, including: Hay fever (allergic rhinitis) Allergic asthma Dermatitis (eczema) Food allergies Penicillin allergy<cover>Bee venom allergy Latex allergy Why see an allergist? Who is an Allergist/Immunologist? An allergist/immunologist (commonly referred to as an allergist) is a physician specially trained to diagnose, treat and manage allergies, asthma and immunologic disorders including primary immunodeficiency disorders. These conditions range from the very common to the very rare, spanning all ages and encompassing various organ systems. In the United States, becoming an allergist / immunologist requires at least an additional nine years of training beyond a bachelor’s degree. When should you see an allergist? If you feel like you’re always getting sick, with a cough or head congestion, it’s time to see an allergist. Often, the symptoms of asthma or allergies develop gradually over time and as a result, your quality of life </cover>becomes poorer. Allergy sufferers may become used to frequent symptoms such as sneezing, nasal congestion or wheezing. With the help of an allergist, these symptoms usually can be prevented or controlled with major improvement in ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_1681810333#2_3172667068","rank":8,"relevance":2},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_32_853332099#10_1643563472","rank":9,"relevance":1},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_1612021813#0_3269334906","rank":10,"relevance":1},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_1004088852#7_1823900839","rank":11,"relevance":3}]}}
