{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"quality of life\" \"venom allergy\" (+ children child adults patients))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_32_853332099#9_1643556350","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1094","rank":4,"snippet":"and <cover>children of all ages, including infants, and results are available immediately. Information obtained from allergy skin tests are most reliable way of diagnosing allergies to airborne substances, such as pollen, pet dander, and dust mites. Skin testing may help diagnose food allergies as well. Allergy skin tests are widely used to help diagnose allergic conditions, including: Hay fever (allergic rhinitis) Allergic asthma Dermatitis (eczema) Food allergies Penicillin allergy Bee venom allergy Latex allergy Why see an allergist? Who is an Allergist/Immunologist? An allergist/immunologist (commonly referred to as an allergist) is a physician specially trained to diagnose, treat and manage allergies, asthma and immunologic disorders including primary immunodeficiency disorders. These conditions range from the very common to the very rare, spanning all ages and encompassing various organ systems. In the United States, becoming an allergist / immunologist requires at least an additional nine years of training beyond a bachelor’s degree. When should you see an allergist? If you feel like you’re always getting sick, with a cough or head congestion, it’s time to see an allergist. Often, the symptoms of asthma or allergies develop gradually over time and as a result, your quality of life </cover>becomes ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_32_853332099#12_1643577752","rank":1,"relevance":1},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_32_853332099#10_1643563472","rank":2,"relevance":1},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_32_853332099#11_1643570592","rank":3,"relevance":1}]}}
