{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"quality of life\" venom allergic patients (+ sting stings bee wasp hymenoptera))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_32_853332099#6_1643534639","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1100","rank":11,"snippet":"<cover>Allergic asthma Dermatitis (eczema) Food allergies Penicillin allergy Bee venom allergy Latex allergy Why see an allergist? Who is an Allergist/Immunologist? An allergist/immunologist (commonly referred to as an allergist) is a physician specially trained to diagnose, treat and manage allergies, asthma and immunologic disorders including primary immunodeficiency disorders. These conditions range from the very common to the very rare, spanning all ages and encompassing various organ systems. In the United States, becoming an allergist / immunologist requires at least an additional nine years of training beyond a bachelor’s degree. When should you see an allergist? If you feel like you’re always getting sick, with a cough or head congestion, it’s time to see an allergist. Often, the symptoms of asthma or allergies develop gradually over time and as a result, your quality of life becomes poorer. Allergy sufferers may become used to frequent symptoms such as sneezing, nasal congestion or wheezing. With the help of an allergist, these symptoms usually can be prevented or controlled with major improvement in quality of life. Our providers have specialized training and can develop a treatment plan for your individual condition. The goal will be to enable you to lead </cover>","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_778278917#16_1605639199","rank":1,"relevance":2},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_03_1067819329#4_1802449718","rank":2,"relevance":2},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_1463836094#8_2825251705","rank":3,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_1463836094#9_2825253470","rank":4,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_32_853332099#12_1643577752","rank":5,"relevance":1},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_778278917#17_1605640225