{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"sting allergy\" \"insect sting allergy\" \"bee sting allergy\") impact quality life)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_1702542043#12_3607001895","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1120","rank":5,"snippet":"Lupus Introduction What are the most common types of allergies? What are the symptoms of an allergic reaction? What causes allergies? Testing for Allergies Preventing and Treating Allergic Reactions Allergies <cover>Impact on Quality of Life In Conclusion References Author: Liz Heintz \" , \"segment\": \"What are the symptoms of an allergic reaction? While the severity and duration can be different from individual to individual, the Mayo Clinic has identified the following most common symptoms of allergic reactions based on the allergen: Allergic Rhinitis (Hay fever): An allergy to plants and pollen can cause sneezing, eye irritation, nasal congestion and an itchy nose or roof of mouth. Food Allergy: Individuals with food allergies may experience swelling (face, tongue, lips, throat), hives, a tingly mouth and even anaphylaxis. Reactions to foods such as wheat, gluten and soy can cause these symptoms along with digestive upset (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea), red and flushed skin, abdominal pain, cramps, headache, nasal congestion and swelling of the face and throat. Insect Sting Allergy: </cover>Insect stings (bees, wasps) can cause hives, redness and swelling around the sting, compromised breathing (wheezing, coughing, tightness of the chest) and anaphylaxis. Drug Allergy:\" , \"start_char","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_1004088852#8_1823903949","rank":1,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_1004088852#7_1823900839","rank":2,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_1702542043#13_3607003456","rank":3,"relevance":1},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_00_193756478#1_336780475","rank":4,"relevance":2}]}}
{"request":{"judgments":"44/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_1702542043#12_3607001895","relevance":1,"reason":"The document is broadly about allergies and lupus, with a general quality-of-life heading and a mention that bee/wasp insect sting allergy can cause hives, swelling, breathing compromise, and anaphylaxis. It does not directly explain bee-sting quality-of-life effects and is not dedicated to the topic.","learned":"Insect sting allergy can cause hives, redness/swelling, compromised breathing, and anaphylaxis, but this source only connects allergies generally—not bee stings specifically—to quality of life."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1112","reason":"The insect-sting-allergies anxiety query produced a dedicated WebMD insect/bee-sting allergy page; continuing it may uncover more dedicated sources that discuss fear, anxiety, anaphylaxis, or other quality-of-life-relevant effects."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1112"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_10_616697030#5_1140802969","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1112","rank":2,"snippet":"one or more of these symptoms at the site of the sting: Pain Redness Pimple-like spots Mild to moderate swelling Warmth Itching Severe allergic reactions (also called an anaphylactic reaction) are not that common. But when they happen, they're emergencies. Symptoms can include: Trouble breathing Hivesthat appear as a red, itchy rash and spread to areas beyond the sting Swelling of the face, throat, or any part of the mouth or tongue Wheezingor trouble swallowing Restlessness and <cover>anxiety Rapid pulse Dizzinessor a sharp drop in blood pressure Get emergency treatment as soon as possible. How Common Are Insect Sting Allergies? About 2 million Americans have allergies to the venom of stinging bugs. Many of these people are at risk for life-threatening allergic reactions. Treatment if You’re Not Allergic First, if you’re stung on the hand, remove any rings from your fingers immediately. If stung by a bee, </cover>the bee usually leaves a sac of venom and a stinger in your skin.\" , \"start_char\": 4315.000000 , \"title\": \"\" , \"url\": \"http://www.webmd.com/allergies/guide/insect-stings\" }"}}
